6.3 User guide (screens, in order, plain language)
The guide is scripted, not written by an LLM. Each screen has text, an image or short animation, and a “Done” or “I need help” button. “I need help” opens the Ask agent, limited to this guide’s content.

Step 1 — Before you start. “This test gives you an early idea. It does not replace a lab test. We show bands, not exact numbers. If anything looks wrong, we will tell you to see a clinician.”

Step 2 — What you need. A checklist with photos: supported lens or microscope, fixed-depth chamber slides, clean sterile container, gloves, a timer, a clean flat table, a bright steady light (for clip-on), tissue and a closed bin bag. Button: “I have a lens”, “I have a microscope”, “I have neither”.

Step 3 — Prepare the sample. (Clinical text to be approved by the clinical advisor; defaults from WHO guidance, VERIFY.)

Abstain from ejaculation for 2 to 7 days before. Enter the number of days.
Wash hands. Collect the sample in a clean, sterile container. No lubricant, no saliva, no condom unless it is a semen-collection condom.
Keep the sample close to body temperature. Do not put it in the fridge or in the sun.
Wait 30 minutes for it to turn thin and watery. Start the test within 1 hour of collection.
Note the time. The app starts a timer and warns if the sample is too old.
Step 4 — Set up the device. Different screens for S-A and S-B, with pictures.

S-A: attach the clip-on lens exactly over the main camera, switch off the torch unless the kit says otherwise, place the chamber, let the app auto-detect that the image is magnified.
S-B: attach the phone to the eyepiece or camera port, choose the objective and condition (phase or bright-field), and complete Calibration (6.5).
Step 5 — Load the chamber. Mix gently. Place one small drop (volume given by the kit) in the chamber. Do not press. Wait for the drop to settle for about 30 seconds.

Step 6 — Find a good field. Live camera view with on-screen guidance: “Move the slide slowly”, “Turn the focus until the small heads look sharp”, “Too dark”, “Too bright”, “Too many cells close together”, “Too few cells, move to another area”. The Capture button turns green only when the quality gate passes (6.6).

Step 7 — Record. The app records short clips, one per field: 5 fields minimum, 10 fields maximum, each 10 seconds, at 30 fps or higher, at least 1080p. After each clip the app says “Move to a new area, the slide must not be the same spot”. It stops early when it has seen enough cells for a reliable band, or when 10 fields are done.

Step 8 — Upload. Resumable upload with a progress bar. If the phone loses signal, the clips stay encrypted on the phone and upload later. The user can close the app.

Step 9 — Result. See 6.9.

Step 10 — Clean-up. Safety text: treat the sample as a biological material. Wear gloves, dispose of the chamber and wipes in a closed bag, clean the lens and table.

Step 11 — Next step. The retest reminder (F14) is offered. A second sample 1 to 2 weeks later is recommended by default for a more dependable picture (clinical advisor to confirm).

6.4 Kit profiles and chamber depth
Concentration needs three facts: how many cells are in view, the area in view and the depth of liquid. The depth comes from the chamber.

semen_kits table stores the known chamber depth (for example 10 µm or 20 µm) for each supported chamber, and default field width.
If the user uses a chamber not in the list, the app asks for the depth printed on the chamber or its box. If the depth is unknown, concentration band = Unclear (reason: depth unknown), and the pipeline still produces a motility band. The chain continues; one result is simply marked Unclear.
An ordinary slide and cover slip (unknown depth) is not accepted for concentration.
6.5 Calibration (needed for S-B, optional check for S-A)
Goal: convert pixels to micrometres, so cell size and speed make sense.

The user photographs a stage micrometer (a slide with a ruler, usually 10 µm divisions) under the same objective and phone setup. If the chamber has a printed grid with a known spacing, that grid can be used.
The server (or the app) detects the line spacing and computes µm per pixel. It stores the value, the objective and the date in the setup profile.
Calibration quality is checked: the spacing detection must find at least 8 lines and the spacing must be consistent within 3%. If not: “Please retake the ruler image.”
If calibration is missing or fails: concentration = Unclear and velocity measures are not used. Motility uses the “moving versus not moving” ratio only and the band is widened. The pipeline still finishes.
A re-calibration prompt appears if the setup profile is older than 30 days or the user changes the phone, adapter or objective.
