6.7 Server pipeline: the stages (the “agents” of the semen flow)
Each stage is an independent worker with one job, one input, one output, one failure state. An orchestrator (a state machine stored in SQL, advanced by Service Bus messages) moves a session from stage to stage. The chain is complete in the sense that every stage always ends in either “passed to next stage” or a defined fallback result, never in silence.

S0 Intake Agent        -> checks consent semen_media, session exists, kit profile, sample metadata (days of abstinence, time since collection)
S1 Setup Agent         -> validates setup profile and calibration, decides which outputs are allowed (conc, motility)
S2 Upload Agent        -> verifies chunk completeness, sha256, duration, fps, resolution
S3 Safety Scan         -> file signature and malware scan; rejects non-video content
S4 Frame Prep Agent    -> decodes video, stabilises, crops to usable circle/field, normalises contrast
S5 Quality Agent       -> repeats the quality gate; per-clip pass, marginal or fail; sets which clips are usable
S6 Detection Agent     -> finds sperm heads per frame (model, or classical fallback)
S7 Tracking Agent      -> links detections into tracks (ByteTrack/OC-SORT); computes VCL, VSL, VAP, STR (if calibrated)
S8 Feature Agent       -> per-field counts, motile fraction, progressive fraction, size sanity, debris ratio, density
S9 Banding Agent       -> turns features into bands with uncertainty (6.8)
S10 Rules Agent        -> combines bands with questionnaire and earlier runs into colour + next step (6.9)
S11 Explanation Agent  -> builds the user text from templates; an LLM may only reword, then a validator checks it
S12 Storage Agent      -> writes results, artifacts, audit, observations; sets retest reminder
S13 Routing Agent      -> if Red or Unclear twice, shows lab options and creates a referral draft for the user to review

